# POST /imap/provision — step 1: the body is JSON {"username": …,
# "password"?: …}.
#
# UNAUTHENTICATED BY DESIGN — a loopback demo. Anyone who can reach the
# web port can create or reset an account under a domain this tenant
# owns. The boundary that always holds is in the op, not this route:
# txco://imap/account runs only inside this tenant's rules and only for
# usernames under a domain the tenant has verified (or serves DNS for).
# WHO may call it is this WHEN clause. A product gates it on its own
# session (a magic-link cookie, an API key checked with txco://hmac-verify)
# or does not expose a route at all and provisions from an internal flow
# (first inbound mail, an owner's claim step). The web inlet stamps the raw body base64 at
# @web.req.body; decode + parse it into a private key for the next scopes,
# and mark that a provision request is in flight (the later "missing
# username" reply gates on the marker, never on the absence of a path).
#
# `username` must be <local>@<domain> where <domain> is a hostname this
# tenant owns (a verified hostname binding or a delegated zone) — the same
# rule txco://sendmail applies to From:. Under `txco dev` the dev-local
# names (localhost, *.localhost, *.local.thanks.computer) are auto-verified
# on bind, so `paris@pony.local.thanks.computer` works once that hostname
# is bound to this stack.
WHEN @src == "http" && @web.req.method == "POST" && @web.req.url.path == "/imap/provision"
  EMIT ._prov = &try_json(&b64decode(@web.req.body)),
       ._prov_req = true
